{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-75832" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table gives the values of functions $g(x)$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({g}^{-1})^{′}(7).$\n\n| **$x$** | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-6$ | $9$ | $-1$ |\n| $4$ | $7$ | $-4$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"} {"question" "q-75832" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=7,$ we have\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(7)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(7))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${g}^{-1}(7)=4$ and ${g}^{′}(4)=-4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({g}^{-1})^{′}(7) & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(7))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"} {"question" "q-75832" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c5dcddb6b4631b43a02ecfd76451d4321f914551d7c41ca3060de13c623213f7"} {"question" "q-75832" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"} {"question" "q-75832" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{7}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"} {"question" "q-75832" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"} {"question" "q-75986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4).$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"} {"question" "q-75986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(0).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(0)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(0) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =0 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $x=0$ since the logarithm is not defined here. Therefore, ${f}^{-1}(0)=5.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(5),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4)+\\frac{x}{x-4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5-4)+\\frac{5}{5-4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"} {"question" "q-75986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c5dcddb6b4631b43a02ecfd76451d4321f914551d7c41ca3060de13c623213f7"} {"question" "q-75986" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"} {"question" "q-75986" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"} {"question" "q-75986" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"} {"question" "q-110284" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(3)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $-3$ |\n| $-2$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $-4$ | $2$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"} {"question" "q-110284" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function ${f}^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(x)=\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}+{g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(3)=\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}+{g}^{′}(3){f}^{-1}(3).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(3)=-2,$ $g(3)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(3)=-3,$ and ${f}^{′}(-2)=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(3) & =\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}+{g}^{′}(3){f}^{-1}(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{f}^{′}(-2)}+(-3)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-5}+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"} {"question" "q-110284" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c5dcddb6b4631b43a02ecfd76451d4321f914551d7c41ca3060de13c623213f7"} {"question" "q-110284" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"} {"question" "q-110284" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-5"] ["math" "-\\frac{44}{5}"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{31}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"} {"question" "q-110284" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{31}{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"} {"question" "q-110347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=-3+5{e}^{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"} {"question" "q-110347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ -3+5{e}^{x+1} & =2 \\\\ 5{e}^{x+1} & =5 \\\\ {e}^{x+1} & =1 \\\\ x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(2)=-1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(-1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-3+5{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =5{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =5{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"} {"question" "q-110347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c5dcddb6b4631b43a02ecfd76451d4321f914551d7c41ca3060de13c623213f7"} {"question" "q-110347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"} {"question" "q-110347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{5}"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"} {"question" "q-110347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"} {"question" "q-312581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x-2}{x+2}$ and the point $P(-1,-5)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(-5,-1)$ is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"} {"question" "q-312581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(-1,-5).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3x-2}{x+2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =8\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(-1,-5)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-5,-1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"} {"question" "q-312581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c5dcddb6b4631b43a02ecfd76451d4321f914551d7c41ca3060de13c623213f7"} {"question" "q-312581" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"} {"question" "q-312581" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{8}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068944/state.json" "file_sha256" "bffe1fcf82506976a384a5cfa7be04036cf42a0301064ce346f7e5cbb7552f10"}] "usage" {"q-75986" []} "basis" 2323 "retractions" [[17592186064864 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = x\\ln (x - 4)$. Given that $y = f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $(f^{-1})^{′}(0)$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186063694 17592186063695 17592186063696 17592186063697 17592186063698] "immutable_fields" [[17592186063693 :answer-field/correct] [17592186063693 :answer-field/id] [17592186063693 :answer-field/key] [17592186063693 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
